{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Mirror inventory finding F12 (`reports/stability/mirror-inventory.md`), HIGH, **ALREADY DRIFTED**.\n\n## Problem\n\nTwo masters for one fact — which subcommands the CLI has and what they exit:\n\n- `src/rebar/_cli/_registry.py` `ROUTES` — 78 live routes, **zero aliases**\n- `docs/exit-codes.md:117` Subcommand table — 81 hand-maintained rows\n\n`docs/exit-codes.md:5-7` claims the file is \"the single source of truth ... pinned by\n`tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_exit_codes.py`, which fails if the codes drift.\" The entire\nassertion touching the file (`:236-243`) is\n`assert \"\\`11\\`\" in text and \"block-but-retryable\" in text.lower()`. Nothing checks the table.\n\n## Measured drift — as the inventory recorded it\n\n**Correction to an earlier revision of this plan.** I briefly recorded four stale rows here.\nThat was wrong: the probes behind it were rewritten by a shell hook that folded all argv into a\nsingle subcommand name, so every multi-token invocation looked non-existent. Re-run against the\nvenv binary directly, `bridge fsck`, `bridge check-access` and `bridge setup` are all REAL — they\nare nested argparse subcommands of the `bridge` route (`rebar bridge --help` lists\n`{preview,run,sync,status,pause,resume,fsck,check-access,setup,projects}`). The inventory's\noriginal reading was correct.\n\nThe drift is exactly two rows, in opposite directions, which is why equal-looking counts hid it:\n\n| side | offender | reality |\n|---|---|---|\n| doc row for a non-command | `review` (`:185`) | removed shim; absent from `ROUTES`, not invocable |\n| live route with no doc row | `bridge-status` | invocable; the hyphenated alias of `bridge status` |\n\nVerified by invoking every one of the 81 documented rows: exactly one, `review`, reports\n`unknown subcommand`.\n\n## Approach — add-census-assertion-to-existing-test\n\nSet-difference the table rows against live `ROUTES` names inside `test_exit_codes.py`, and correct\nthe five discrepancies.\n\nPlain set equality would be WRONG, and that is the substance of this ticket. `ROUTES` holds\nTOP-LEVEL route names; the table documents user-facing INVOCATIONS, which include a route's\nnested subcommands. Equality would fail on `bridge setup` and friends — real, working, correctly\ndocumented commands — which is exactly the concept collapse the epic's AC2 forbids. The invariant\nis two-directional but asymmetric: every live route must be documented under SOME spelling, and\nevery documented row must BEGIN with a live route name.\n\nThis does not collapse a distinct concept (epic AC2): the table's Subcommand column and `ROUTES`\nalready denote the same set — the invocable subcommands — and the table's extra columns (exit\ncodes per command) stay hand-authored and unconstrained.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: `test_exit_codes.py` asserts the ASYMMETRIC invariant, comparing real parsed values\n      rather than re-listed literals: every live non-retired `ROUTES` name is documented under\n      some spelling, and every table row BEGINS with a live route name. Plain set equality is\n      explicitly NOT the invariant — it would fail on the real nested subcommands (`bridge setup`,\n      `bridge fsck`, `bridge check-access`). Verified by the test passing after the doc is fixed.\n- [ ] AC2: the assertion fails in BOTH directions and names the offenders. Verified by two\n      mutation checks — an extra doc row, and a route removed from the compared set — each RED\n      with the offending name in the message.\n- [ ] AC3: the one non-invocable row, `review`, is removed. (An earlier revision of this plan\n      claimed four such rows; that came from probes mangled by a shell hook and was withdrawn —\n      the other three are real nested subcommands.) Verified by invoking every documented row\n      through the venv binary directly and finding none that report `unknown subcommand`.\n- [ ] AC4: `bridge-status` gains a row documenting its exit behavior. Verified by AC1's equality.\n- [ ] AC5: the false pinning claim at `:5-7` is corrected to describe what the test now actually\n      enforces. Verified by reading it against the assertions.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `python -m pytest tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_exit_codes.py -q` — the census assertion and\n  its drift cases (AC1, AC2), green only once the doc is corrected (AC3, AC4).\n- `for c in <every row>; do rebar $c --help; done` — proves no documented row is non-invocable\n  (AC3); the census test encodes this so it does not rely on a manual sweep.\n- `make lint && make typecheck`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788381582419419001,"uuid":"3c6e41fe-02da-46d0-9119-e31a33558f68"}